 period_rationale="Selected milestones expand from 1976–83 (Walker, Ward, Avery, Abella and Troper, Perin), i.e. 'Reorientations, c. 1970–85', and continue into period_3 (Anderson 1991, Iacovetta 1992, Walker 1997, Backhouse 1999). Trudel (1960) and Winks (1971) are earlier witnesses. Matches groups.json (period_2/period_3).",
 date_label="Earlier work (Trudel 1960; Winks 1971) · Walker 1976 · Ward 1978 · Avery 1979 · Abella and Troper 1982 · Perin 1983 · Anderson 1991 · Walker 1997 · Backhouse 1999 · selected publication milestones, not boundaries; coverage through 2000",
 date_span=dict(start=1960, start_kind='earlier_roots', end=1999, end_kind='unstated', precision='year',
  basis='Selected publication milestones only. 1960 and 1971 are earlier witnesses, not a field origin; 1999 is the last selected in-period milestone. Post-2000 work sits in extension_coverage.'),
 description=("Studies immigration, ethnic communities, racism and the state in Canada: immigrant workers and their reception, ethnic identity, and the legal and spatial making of racial categories. "
  "Earlier works on slavery in New France (Trudel 1960) and on Black Canadians (Winks 1971) preceded an expansion from the mid-1970s. "
  "Walker's The Black Loyalists (1976) placed Black community at the centre; Ward's White Canada Forever (1978) traced west-coast prejudice against Asian residents and the discriminatory policies it produced; Avery's 'Dangerous Foreigners' (1979) examined European immigrant workers and labour radicalism; Abella and Troper studied Canada and the Jews of Europe, 1933–48 (1982). "
  "Perin's 'Clio as an Ethnic' (1983) asked whether ethnic groups had been 'discovered' just when the state faced challenges such as Quebec nationalism. "
  "Studies of Toronto neighbourhoods (Harney), Italian migrants in Montreal (Ramirez), Chinese Montrealers (Helly), British Columbia politicians (Roy), Italian immigrants in postwar Toronto (Iacovetta) and Ukrainian-Canadian women (Swyripa) followed. "
  "Anderson (1987, 1991) treated 'Chinese' and 'Chinatown' as racial categories made through government practice and place; Backhouse's Colour-Coded (1999) traced racism through law. "
  "Walker distinguished five orientations in African-Canadian historiography, from Black 'clients' of white philanthropy to Black community and 'survivors' (1997). "
  "Post-2000 work turned to Cold War reception and citizenship (Iacovetta 2006; Bohaker and Iacovetta 2009)."),
 scope_note="A research field joining immigration and ethnic history with histories of racism and Black Canadian history. Black Canadian history is included here as a strand because the request listed no separate entry (see open questions); it is not a subfield of immigration history. Indigenous–settler relations are in native_newcomer and settler_colonialism. Roster inclusion is not adoption of one framework: Ward's 'attitudes and public policy' and Anderson's 'racial category' approaches differ.",
 rfw=("Earlier: Trudel, L'esclavage au Canada français (1960); Winks, The Blacks in Canada (1971). "
  "Walker, The Black Loyalists (1976); Ward, White Canada Forever (1978); Avery, \"Dangerous Foreigners\" (1979); Abella and Troper, None Is Too Many (1982); Palmer, Patterns of Prejudice (1982); Perin, \"Clio as an Ethnic\" (1983); Ramirez, Les premiers Italiens de Montréal (1984) and On the Move (1991); Harney, ed., Gathering Place (1985); Anderson, \"The Idea of Chinatown\" (1987) and Vancouver's Chinatown (1991); Helly, Les Chinois à Montréal (1987); Roy, A White Man's Province (1989); Iacovetta, Such Hardworking People (1992) and \"Manly Militants\" (1995); Knowles, Strangers at Our Gates (1992); Swyripa, Wedded to the Cause (1993); Avery, Reluctant Host (1995); Walker, \"Allegories and Orientations\" (1997) and \"Race,\" Rights and the Law (1997); Backhouse, Colour-Coded (1999). "
  "Extension: Epp, Iacovetta and Swyripa, eds., Sisters or Strangers? (2004); Iacovetta, Gatekeepers (2006); Bohaker and Iacovetta (2009)."),
